        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Ectopic tubal pregnancy, surgically treated</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 75-year-old female patient, admitted on 28th May 1998 with confirmed tubal ectopic pregnancy. Upon admission, clinical examination revealed lower abdominal pain with signs of early haemodynamic compromise. Transvaginal ultrasonography confirmed a right-sided tubal ectopic pregnancy without evidence of rupture. Serial serum beta-hCG measurements supported the diagnosis. Given the patient's age and haemodynamic status, surgical intervention was deemed necessary. The patient underwent laparoscopic salpingectomy with successful removal of the ectopic pregnancy. The postoperative course was uneventful. Vital signs remained stable throughout recovery. The patient was mobilised early with physiotherapy support and discharged in good general condition on 8th June 1998. Please arrange follow-up within two weeks. I remain available for any further queries.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
